1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 6.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,850
4, App Control for Business.

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,700
Now you control which programs may run at
all.

4
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:14,350
You will learn the difference between an
allow list and a block list, and when to

5
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:16,600
pick App Control or AppLocker.

6
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:23,250
You will read a policy file and start from
an example policy that ships with Windows.

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:29,100
You will meet policy formats, deploy a
policy with CiTool or Group Policy, read

8
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:34,150
the audit events, and remove a policy
safely, all in audit mode.

9
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,200
There are two ways to control
applications.

10
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:41,450
A block list allows everything except the
files you name.

11
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:46,500
Brand new malware is not on the list yet,
so it runs.

12
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:49,950
An allow list works the other way around.

13
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,000
Only files the policy trusts may run, so
unknown code is stopped.

14
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:59,650
When a program starts, Windows asks: does
a rule trust it?

15
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:01,500
If yes, it runs.

16
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:06,150
If no, an enforced policy blocks it and
logs event 3077.

17
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:11,200
In audit mode it still runs, and Windows
logs event 3076 instead.

18
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:13,850
Windows has two application control
technologies.

19
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:17,300
App Control for Business arrived with
Windows 10.

20
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:20,750
Its old name was Windows Defender
Application Control.

21
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,200
Microsoft services it as a security
feature, it covers kernel drivers, and it

22
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:28,050
keeps getting new features.

23
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:32,300
Its policies apply to the whole device and
every user.

24
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:34,950
AppLocker is older, from Windows 7.

25
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:40,400
It only gets security fixes, but it can
target specific users and groups.

26
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:46,650
So the rule of thumb: use App Control
where you can, and add AppLocker when

27
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:50,500
different people on a shared PC need
different rules.

28
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:53,950
An App Control policy is an XML file.

29
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:58,200
At the top you see the PolicyID and the
BasePolicyID.

30
00:01:58,450 --> 00:02:04,850
They are GUIDs, long unique numbers, and
for a base policy both hold the same GUID.

31
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:07,100
Next comes the Rules section.

32
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:12,950
These are rule options, switches for the
whole policy, such as Enabled colon Audit

33
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,400
Mode.

34
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:17,450
Then come the file rules and signers.

35
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:21,700
They say which files or publishers are
trusted, or denied.

36
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:27,550
Near the end, the Settings section holds
the friendly name that tools like CiTool

37
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:29,000
show.

38
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:31,650
PowerShell cmdlets edit it for you.

39
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:33,500
Rule options have numbers.

40
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:38,950
Option 0, Enabled colon UMCI, means the
policy also checks user-mode programs, not

41
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:40,400
only drivers.

42
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:47,050
Option 3, Enabled colon Audit Mode, is the
one to remember: it logs what would be

43
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:48,900
blocked, and blocks nothing.

44
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:52,750
Option 6 lets the policy file itself stay
unsigned.

45
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,800
Option 11 turns off checks of scripts.

46
00:02:56,050 --> 00:03:02,050
Option 16 lets later policy updates apply
without a restart, and option 17 lets a

47
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,300
base policy accept supplemental policies.

48
00:03:04,550 --> 00:03:09,750
Set-RuleOption with dash Option 3 adds
audit mode; adding dash Delete removes it,

49
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,600
which enforces the policy.

50
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:15,850
A rule level is how a rule recognizes a
file.

51
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:18,500
Hash trusts the exact file contents.

52
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:21,150
Every update needs a new rule.

53
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:23,800
FilePath trusts a folder or path.

54
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:26,450
Use it only for admin-only folders.

55
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:33,100
At run time App Control ignores a path
rule for a folder that normal users can

56
00:03:33,350 --> 00:03:36,150
write to, like C colon Users Public.

57
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:41,600
Publisher trusts a signing certificate and
company name, so all of that company's

58
00:03:41,850 --> 00:03:43,050
signed software.

59
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:47,700
FilePublisher narrows it to one file name
and a minimum version.

60
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:51,550
New-CIPolicy picks levels with dash Level
and dash Fallback.

61
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:55,800
Windows ships example policies in C colon
Windows, schemas, CodeIntegrity,

62
00:03:56,050 --> 00:03:57,250
ExamplePolicies.

63
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:01,100
DefaultWindows comes in an audit and an
enforced version.

64
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:07,350
It allows Windows itself, kernel drivers
and Store apps, and it is our lab policy.

65
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,800
AllowMicrosoft adds apps signed by
Microsoft, like Office.

66
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:15,050
AllowAll allows everything, the starting
point for a block list.

67
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:18,900
There is also an example supplemental
policy for DefaultWindows.

68
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:24,750
Microsoft also offers a graphical App
Control Wizard, but it is a download, and

69
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
our pod has no internet.

70
00:04:27,250 --> 00:04:29,250
Policies come in two formats.

71
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:35,100
The old single policy format allows one
policy per computer, in a file called

72
00:04:35,350 --> 00:04:36,550
SiPolicy dot p7b.

73
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:42,400
The multiple policy format, from Windows
10 1903 and in Windows 11, lets many

74
00:04:42,650 --> 00:04:49,050
policies work side by side, each in a file
named after its policy ID, dot cip.

75
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:53,700
Two base policies form an intersection: a
file must pass both.

76
00:04:53,950 --> 00:04:59,150
A supplemental policy adds trust to a
base: a file passes either one.

77
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:02,200
The base needs option 17 for that.

78
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:06,850
Set-CIPolicyIdInfo links a supplemental to
its base, and Merge-CIPolicy joins rules.

79
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:10,300
Here are the first lab commands on
CLIENT01.

80
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:15,350
Make a folder, C colon AppControl, and
copy DefaultWindows underscore Audit dot

81
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:19,200
xml into it as SA1 underscore Audit dot
xml.

82
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:23,450
Then Set-CIPolicyIdInfo with dash
ResetPolicyID gives the copy its own

83
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:27,300
unique ID and puts it in multiple policy
format.

84
00:05:27,550 --> 00:05:31,150
Dash PolicyName sets the friendly name,
SA1 Audit CLIENT01.

85
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:35,800
Set-RuleOption with dash Option 3 makes
sure audit mode is on.

86
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:40,850
Finally, list the rule options and check
that audit mode is there.

87
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:43,900
How does a policy reach a computer?

88
00:05:44,150 --> 00:05:49,750
CiTool, with dash dash update-policy, is
built into Windows 11 22H2 and later and

89
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,200
Windows Server 2025.

90
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:57,050
Group Policy has a setting called Deploy
App Control for Business, but it supports

91
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:01,300
only the single policy format, and a
restart applies it.

92
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:05,550
Intune uses a configuration service
provider that handles multiple policies

93
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:07,000
without a restart.

94
00:06:07,250 --> 00:06:11,650
Our policy is in multiple policy format,
so CLIENT01 uses CiTool.

95
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:15,500
You still learn where the Group Policy
setting lives.

96
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:18,950
Windows loads a binary file, not the XML.

97
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:23,600
First, read the policy ID from the XML
into a variable.

98
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:28,650
The binary file name is that ID with the
extension dot cip.

99
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:31,700
ConvertFrom-CIPolicy turns the XML into
that binary.

100
00:06:31,950 --> 00:06:36,350
Then CiTool dash dash update-policy
deploys it, and it needs administrator

101
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:37,800
rights.

102
00:06:38,050 --> 00:06:44,450
To check, run CiTool dash lp with dash
json, convert the JSON, and filter on the

103
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:45,900
friendly name.

104
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:49,750
IsEnforced True means the policy is loaded
and active.

105
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:56,800
Do not let the word fool you: it is still
an audit mode policy, so it blocks

106
00:06:57,050 --> 00:06:58,250
nothing.

107
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:00,900
Here is the Group Policy way.

108
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:05,550
In the Group Policy Management Editor, go
to Computer Configuration, Policies,

109
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,800
Administrative Templates, System, Device
Guard.

110
00:07:08,050 --> 00:07:10,450
Open Deploy App Control for Business.

111
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:16,700
Select Enabled, then type the path of the
policy file in the Code Integrity Policy

112
00:07:16,950 --> 00:07:18,150
file path box.

113
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:20,400
Now remember the two limits.

114
00:07:20,650 --> 00:07:26,650
This setting takes only a single format
policy file, and computers apply it at the

115
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:28,100
next restart.

116
00:07:28,350 --> 00:07:35,150
That is why the lab only looks at this
setting on DC01 and does not use it.

117
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,800
App Control reports in Event Viewer.

118
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:41,250
Open Applications and Services Logs,
Microsoft, Windows, CodeIntegrity,

119
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:42,700
Operational.

120
00:07:42,950 --> 00:07:45,750
Event 3076 is the main audit event.

121
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:51,200
It means this file would have been blocked
if the policy were enforced.

122
00:07:51,450 --> 00:07:55,450
Event 3089 adds the signature details of
that same file.

123
00:07:55,700 --> 00:08:01,700
Event 3099 means a policy was loaded, so
you can confirm your policy is active.

124
00:08:01,950 --> 00:08:04,750
The fourth number to know is 3077.

125
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,000
That one means an enforced policy actually
blocked a file.

126
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:14,850
Because we stay in audit mode, you will
not see 3077 in the lab.

127
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:18,300
You can read the same log with PowerShell.

128
00:08:18,550 --> 00:08:20,950
Get-WinEvent reads the CodeIntegrity
Operational log.

129
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:26,400
Filter for IDs 3076 and 3077, and group
them to count each one.

130
00:08:26,650 --> 00:08:30,250
The count differs on every computer, which
is normal.

131
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:34,100
There is no 3077 line, because nothing was
blocked.

132
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:40,350
Then filter for event 3076 with a message
that contains hello dot exe, our unsigned

133
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:43,400
test program, and show the first one.

134
00:08:43,650 --> 00:08:49,650
The message names the file in C colon
Users Public that would have been blocked.

135
00:08:49,900 --> 00:08:51,500
First, stop the deployment.

136
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:57,750
If a Group Policy Object or an Intune
profile still deploys the policy, it comes

137
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:59,200
right back.

138
00:08:59,450 --> 00:09:03,850
Second, remove it with CiTool dash rp and
the policy ID.

139
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:06,900
Third, restart if your version needs it.

140
00:09:07,150 --> 00:09:12,750
From the Windows 11 2024 update on, an
unsigned policy goes away without a

141
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:14,200
restart.

142
00:09:14,450 --> 00:09:19,250
Fourth, check with CiTool dash lp that SA1
Audit CLIENT01 is gone.

143
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:23,100
One big warning: never just delete a
signed policy.

144
00:09:23,350 --> 00:09:25,350
Windows can fail to start.

145
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:29,600
You deploy a signed replacement first, as
Microsoft Learn describes.

146
00:09:29,850 --> 00:09:31,450
Four mistakes to avoid.

147
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:35,700
One: deploying a new allow list straight
in enforced mode.

148
00:09:35,950 --> 00:09:41,950
Start in audit mode, read the 3076 events,
fix the policy, and only then enforce.

149
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:45,800
Two: trusting a user-writable folder with
a FilePath rule.

150
00:09:46,050 --> 00:09:51,650
App Control ignores that rule at run time,
so use Publisher or FilePublisher rules.

151
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:56,700
Three: pushing a multiple-format dot cip
file with the Group Policy setting.

152
00:09:56,950 --> 00:09:59,750
That setting takes the single format only.

153
00:09:60,000 --> 00:10:04,800
Four: deleting a policy while a GPO or
Intune still deploys it.

154
00:10:05,050 --> 00:10:09,050
Turn off the deployment first, then remove
it with CiTool.

155
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:10,900
Time to check yourself.

156
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:15,150
CLIENT01 runs the policy SA1 Audit
CLIENT01, made from DefaultWindows

157
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:16,600
underscore Audit.

158
00:10:16,850 --> 00:10:20,450
First, you see event 3076 for hello dot
exe.

159
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:24,300
Did it run, and what would event 3077
mean?

160
00:10:24,550 --> 00:10:30,550
Second, which rule option makes a policy
audit only, and how do you enforce it

161
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,000
later?

162
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:37,850
Third, why deploy with CiTool and not with
the Deploy App Control for Business

163
00:10:38,100 --> 00:10:39,700
setting in a GPO?

164
00:10:39,950 --> 00:10:44,750
And fourth, students and staff share a PC
and need different rules.

165
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,600
What do you add?

166
00:10:46,850 --> 00:10:49,650
Pause the video and answer all four.

167
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:52,300
One: yes, hello dot exe ran.

168
00:10:52,550 --> 00:10:58,950
3076 is an audit event, so it would have
been blocked only if the policy were

169
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:00,400
enforced.

170
00:11:00,650 --> 00:11:03,850
3077 means an enforced policy really
blocked it.

171
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:06,900
Two: option 3, Enabled colon Audit Mode.

172
00:11:07,150 --> 00:11:12,350
Remove it with Set-RuleOption dash Option
3 dash Delete, convert again and redeploy,

173
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:14,200
but only after testing.

174
00:11:14,450 --> 00:11:20,050
Three: the policy is in multiple policy
format, and the Group Policy setting takes

175
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:21,500
single format only.

176
00:11:21,750 --> 00:11:27,350
Four: add AppLocker on top of App Control,
because AppLocker rules can target users

177
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:28,800
and groups.

178
00:11:29,050 --> 00:11:30,250
Now on CLIENT01.

179
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:37,300
I make two test programs in C colon Users
Public: a copy of whoami, which keeps its

180
00:11:37,550 --> 00:11:41,150
Microsoft signature, and hello dot exe,
which nobody signed.

181
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:47,000
I copy DefaultWindows Audit, reset its ID,
name it SA1 Audit CLIENT01, and confirm

182
00:11:47,250 --> 00:11:48,450
audit mode.

183
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:55,900
I convert it to a dot cip file and deploy
it with CiTool, and it shows as active.

184
00:11:56,150 --> 00:11:57,750
Both programs still run.

185
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:03,200
In Event Viewer, under CodeIntegrity
Operational, I find a 3076 event for hello

186
00:12:03,450 --> 00:12:06,650
dot exe, but none for the whoami copy.

187
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:09,700
Finally, I remove the policy with CiTool.

188
00:12:09,950 --> 00:12:15,150
An allow list stops unknown code, and you
always start in audit mode.

189
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:19,000
Start from an example such as
DefaultWindows underscore Audit.

190
00:12:19,250 --> 00:12:24,450
Rule options switch the policy's modes,
and rule levels decide how files are

191
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:25,900
matched.

192
00:12:26,150 --> 00:12:31,750
The multiple policy format uses policy ID
dot cip files, with base and supplemental

193
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:33,200
policies.

194
00:12:33,450 --> 00:12:37,450
Deploy with CiTool, Intune, or Group
Policy for single format.

195
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:43,300
Event 3076 means would block, 3077 means
blocked, and CiTool dash rp removes a

196
00:12:43,550 --> 00:12:44,750
policy.

197
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,200
In Lab 6.

198
00:12:46,450 --> 00:12:52,050
4, about 60 minutes and practice only, you
do all of this on CLIENT01.
